{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: after a local peak/spike in history, the model extrapolates strength, but the market mean-reverts and often sells off. Add a regime rule: if last 5–10 days show an upswing/spike, dampen the forecast slope and pull levels back toward the 20–30d mean.",
    "Overshoot on declines: when the true series enters a sustained downtrend, the model reacts too slowly and stays near the prior range (≈70–74) while outcomes fall into mid-60s. Introduce faster downward adjustment: if recent momentum is negative, subtract an extra penalty that grows with cumulative negative return.",
    "Horizon-shape error: forecasts are too flat/high at the back end (days 15–30) and fail to capture large drawdowns. Enforce stronger mean reversion at longer horizons by blending progressively more weight to long-run mean / recent realized trend rather than the model path.",
    "Volatility underestimation in direction: model keeps prices within a narrow high band while realized outcomes exhibit larger directional moves downward. Add a directional volatility scaler: expand downside moves more than upside moves when macro/trend regime is bearish."
  ]
}
